    infrastructure ([38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no test script).

  That's the one thing I need from you before designing. The fork: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m gets a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter, but the only caller
  has nothing to pass it — so the answer determines whether this stays a one-line signature change or pulls in a new
  source of identity.

  - [1mClient generates it[0m — the submit handler creates an id (e.g. [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m) and passes it in. Buys you a
    correlation id for this login attempt today, with no backend dependency. Costs: it identifies the [3mattempt[0m, not the
    person — two logins from the same human get different ids. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mComes from the server[0m — the real account id, which only the API knows. That means [38;5;153mlogin()[39m can't take it as an input;
    it would come back in the response instead. Rules out the parameter shape you asked for, and is the expensive one
    to get wrong, since anything you log against the client-side id would have to be re-keyed.
  - [1mCaller supplies it, source TBD[0m — add the parameter, thread it through as optional, leave the call site passing [38;5;153mnull[39m
    until something upstream produces one. Buys an unblocked signature; costs a parameter that is dead on arrival.

  My recommendation is the first one, given this is a stub with no backend: a client-generated id is the only option
  that actually works end-to-end today, and it's trivial to swap for a server id once the API is real.
